  ✓ CONFIRMED: Legacy router ignores minimum amounts, enabling reserve-pric
    Justification: Upon reviewing VaderRouter.sol:addLiquidity and 
BasePool.sol:mint, I confirmed that the core slippage vulnerability exists. The 
Uniswap-compatible router overload explicitly declares amountAMin and amountBMin
but discards both values when forwarding to the six-argument addLiquidity 
function. That function applies only ensure(deadline), calls _addLiquidity, and 
_addLiquidity returns amountADesired and amountBDesired unconditionally rather 
than calculating amounts against current reserves or enforcing minimums. Both 
desired amounts are then transferred directly to the pool before pool.mint(to) 
executes. BasePool.mint derives liquidity from the current reserves and 
deposited balances through VaderMath.calculateLiquidityUnits, including a 
reserve-sensitive slip adjustment. Consequently, an attacker can trade against 
the pool before the victim transaction, alter the reserve ratio, and cause the 
victim’s fixed two-asset deposit to mint fewer liquidity units, effectively 
donating value to existing liquidity providers, before trading back. The 
deadline check is present and mitigates expiration, but it does not address 
reserve manipulation or minimum-output protection. VaderRouterV2 contains the 
same ignored-minimum pattern.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Direct VADER donations manipulate XVader share price and ena
    Justification: Upon reviewing the XVader contract, I confirmed that the 
donation-based share-price manipulation is exploitable. In enter(), the contract
calculates totalVader using vader.balanceOf(address(this)) and derives new 
shares as (_amount * totalShares) / totalVader, while the VADER transfer occurs 
afterward. Because any holder can transfer VADER directly to XVader, an attacker
can first acquire shares, donate VADER to increase the observed balance, and 
then front-run or precede a victim deposit. The victim may receive substantially
fewer shares or zero shares due to integer division, with no minimum-share or 
zero-share check. In leave(), redemption is likewise based on the entire live 
VADER balance, so the attacker’s pre-existing shares can withdraw value 
attributable to the victim’s deposit. For example, with one initial share, a 
sufficiently large donation can make a subsequent victim deposit mint zero 
shares, after which the attacker can redeem the original share for the full 
balance. No internal asset accounting, donation exclusion, virtual liquidity, 
slippage protection, or minimum-mint requirement exists in the reviewed 
implementation.


